//! The board that ships with the game: Green TD v36.0 (`Sencility`), 96x96.
//!
//! Every number here was read out of the real map rather than invented, and
//! the provenance matters because the geometry *is* the game design:
//!
//!   wc3maps.com/map/153802  ->  `Green TD 36.0.w3x`
//!     war3map.j          the region rectangles and the enter-region triggers
//!     war3map.w3e        the 96 x 96 tile grid, 128 units per tile
//!
//! The original moves a creep the only way a Warcraft III map can without a
//! custom movement system: a trigger fires when a unit enters a region and
//! orders it to "move" to the *centre of the next region*. The map is
//! therefore a graph of waypoint rectangles, and this file is that graph
//! flattened into one polyline per lane.
//!
//! Coordinates are Warcraft III world units. The playable area is
//! -6144..6144 on both axes, so the origin is the middle of the board.
//!
//! # The shape of a match
//!
//! Ten lanes -- nine colours, with red holding two spawns so that the middle
//! player is attacked from both sides -- run from a spawn on the rim, through
//! the middle of the board, and into one shared funnel at the bottom centre
//! where they merge. The last point of every lane is the goal.
//!
//! The original spawns every lane on every wave, whether or not a human holds
//! that colour, which is why a solo game is attacked from all ten directions.
//! `sim::waves` reproduces that: a wave is spawned on every lane the map has.
//!
//! # Deliberate simplifications
//!
//! * Each lane is a polyline that repeats the shared funnel, rather than ten
//!   lanes sharing one segment. The original re-orders a creep on entering
//!   the next region, so the funnel is genuinely shared; a polyline per lane
//!   is what the current sim can follow (`creep.dist` along one path), and it
//!   keeps the visible behaviour: the creeps meet at the funnel and reach the
//!   same goal. What it costs: lane *lengths* differ (13k to 32k units), so
//!   "furthest along its lane" is only an approximation of "closest to the
//!   goal" for creeps in different lanes. Named as a policy in `sim-010`.
//! * `Path_1d` and `Path_2d` are dead triggers in the original (they register
//!   on regions that do not exist) and are not reproduced.
//! * `gg_rct_leftgo3` has a trigger that fires on `leftgo4` -- the author's
//!   naming, not a missing step. The chain below follows the *regions*, which
//!   is what the engine did.
//!
//! # Zones
//!
//! The nine `*Zone` rectangles are kept because they tile the board and are
//! what the ground is drawn from. They are **decoration**: the original
//! declares them and never reads them, so nothing here restricts building to
//! a player's own zone. That is a decision a later task can revisit with the
//! evidence in hand rather than a rule this file smuggles in.
(
    id: "green_td_v36",
    name: "Green TD v36.0",
    // war3map.w3e: 97x97 tilepoints, i.e. a 96x96 board of 128-unit tiles.
    tiles_x: 96,
    tiles_y: 96,
    tile_size: 128.0,
    // A cell with any part of a lane within this radius is not buildable:
    // the original has the same "no maze, no blocking" rule, and 0.95 of
    // a tile keeps a tower off the path it is shooting across.
    path_clearance: 121.6,
    goal: (
        name: "END",
        x: 0.0,
        y: -5584.0,
        half_w: 96.0,
        half_h: 144.0,
    ),
    lanes: [
